 000  facial recognition cameras are for our  safety but actually they're collecting  data  even the moscow mayor confirms from that  database it is clear who you spend time  with and for how long your social  circles will be identified that  information can definitely be used for  political purposes and against kremlin  critics  in moscow the reactions to the idea of  the facial recognition system on the  subway are mixed  we're losing control while it's becoming  easier for the government to control us  it's logical to have this system because  there is the danger of terrorist attacks  so i am in favor of it  i am conservative i guess i don't want  to sign up to face pay it's scary  face pay test passenger vladimir yakov  plans to keep using the system now that  it's being rolled out and he's not too  concerned about privacy issues  there's cameras everywhere already so i  don't think it's a big deal it doesn't  scare me  moscow's transport authority has  promised that for now traditional  payment methods for the city's metro  system will remain in use but they say  there may soon be a discount for anyone  who opts to pay using their biometric  data  i'm joined now by nasser dahmer a cyber  security expert from the fraunhofer  institute thank you for taking the time  to speak to dw now as we saw in that  report mixed reaction from people  technology prompts unique questions  mostly about privacy concerns now many  rights groups have voiced their concern  particularly with this facial  recognition and what that could mean in  terms of disadvantages for people what's  the most pressing concern that you have  so there are uh two main issues uh  regarding their privacy in this matter  is to use this system um in an unconsent  and out of regulation reasons and the  second part is that  the data the biometric data the facial  images are stored um in link with uh  identity the name for example  and  this link can be breached  even though it's it's it's secured and  this 